, 							{
								"box" : 								{
									"bgcolor" : [ 0.490196078431373, 0.650980392156863, 0.890196078431372, 1.0 ],
									"id" : "obj-53",
									"linecount" : 4,
									"maxclass" : "comment",
									"numinlets" : 1,
									"numoutlets" : 0,
									"patching_rect" : [ 1309.5, 731.0, 150.0, 60.0 ],
									"text" : "Tanh also is also a Waveshaper adding harmonic content to your signal = Drive"
								}

							}
, 							{
								"box" : 								{
									"id" : "obj-57",
									"linecount" : 15,
									"maxclass" : "comment",
									"numinlets" : 1,
									"numoutlets" : 0,
									"patching_rect" : [ 36.0, 1037.0, 1010.0, 221.0 ],
									"text" : "Aesthetic and Generative Justification:\n\nWhat makes it \"ear-catching\" but not annoying?\n\nThe combination of the FM-Pulsar provides a sound that is rich in harmonics (ear-catching) but uses cosine windowing and stepped, rhythmic control (not annoying). The slower inputs (hour, day) control the fundamental elements (harmony and texture depth), ensuring stability, while the faster inputs (seconds, minute) handle subtle variations in spectral color and pulse density, preventing stagnation.\n\nHow did you make the variations consistently interesting, not too much the same, and yet still of a similar character?\nThe character is held constant by the FM-Pulsar synthesis method itself. It's always a windowed pulse being modulated, guaranteeing a consistent harmonic fingerprint.\nLong-Term Interest (hour, day): These parameters dictate the major changes. The hourly shift in root note and the daily change in FM depth are large enough to fundamentally change the sonic mood, ensuring that the station is \"not too much the same\" over weeks.\nMomentary Interest (second, minute): These parameters only control timbre (window width) and rhythm (pulse density). They make the music evolve minute-by-minute without ever changing the current key or fundamental texture.\nThe result is a station that feels familiar within a given hour but undergoes a profound transformation across the span of days.\n"
								}

							}
, 							{
								"box" : 								{
									"id" : "obj-52",
									"linecount" : 5,
									"maxclass" : "comment",
									"numinlets" : 1,
									"numoutlets" : 0,
									"patching_rect" : [ 535.0, 10.0, 739.0, 74.0 ],
									"text" : "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReferences (examples in Max)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n- Max Gen examples\n- Book \"Programming Sound in Max\" example go.* abstractions used as conceptual starting points."
								}

							}
, 							{
								"box" : 								{
									"id" : "obj-35",
									"linecount" : 13,
									"maxclass" : "comment",
									"numinlets" : 1,
									"numoutlets" : 0,
									"patching_rect" : [ 36.0, 837.0, 1010.0, 194.0 ],
									"text" : "1. Chosen Generative Modules and Modifications\nI selected three distinct sound engines, drawing from common DSP techniques available in gen~ and the textbook's go. abstractions, to create rhythmic, melodic, and ambient layers.\nMy design is built on layered synthesis, combining rhythmic pulsing with harmonic modulation, ensuring both an \"upbeat\" feel and long-term listening endurance.\n\nThis radio uses time elements to change the sound at different rates. The principle is: The fast parameter (seconds, minutes) controls the texture/timbral, while the slow parameter (hours, days, months) controls the harmony/nature.seconds → Pulse Density Control: This changes the speed range of the audio pulses (Pulse Rate) every minute. This makes the sound texture change constantly, sometimes denser, sometimes sparser.minute → Window Width Control: This parameter adjusts the Format of the pulse, i.e. the timbre of the sound. As the hour passes, the bell/click can change from warm and dim to bright and sharp.hour → Pitch Control: This is the most important factor. It sets the tone (Root Note) for the entire background cycle. The sound only changes harmony every hour, creating stability for long-time listeners.day → Timbre Depth Control: This parameter controls the level of Frequency Modulation (FM Index) of the oscillator. It changes the complexity, the \"rumble\" of the sound every day, ensuring that today sounds different from yesterday.month → Global Effect Control (e.g., Panning/Reverb): This is the slowest change, used to create a difference in the sound space over the months (e.g., shifting the sound position to the left/right), avoiding boredom when listening for a very long time.bar / beat: These phasors are used to synchronize everything. They ensure any changes (e.g.Sample-and-Hold for Pitch or Timbre) are locked to the beat, keeping the radio station feeling upbeat and rhythmically clear, not completely random.\n"
								}

							}
, 							{
