												}

											}
, 											{
												"box" : 												{
													"id" : "obj-19",
													"maxclass" : "newobj",
													"numinlets" : 0,
													"numoutlets" : 1,
													"outlettype" : [ "" ],
													"patching_rect" : [ 216.5, 17.0, 105.0, 22.0 ],
													"text" : "in 1 ramp-unipolar"
												}

											}
, 											{
												"box" : 												{
													"id" : "obj-18",
													"linecount" : 39,
													"maxclass" : "comment",
													"numinlets" : 1,
													"numoutlets" : 0,
													"patching_rect" : [ 570.0, 17.0, 358.0, 529.0 ],
													"text" : "The output is a ramp that follows the input ramp (in 1) with a divided (or multiplied) rate (in 2).  \n\nAt the core of a ramp-based clock divider, we take the rate of change of the incoming phasor (ramp2slope), and integrate this slope (via the history, +, wrap loop). \n\nIf incoming phasor graudally changes slope, our derived slope also changes accordingly. If the incoming phasor jumps, our derived slope also causes a jump. Negative ramps and divisions work too. \n\nChanging divisions that are not simple integer ratios can cause the follower ramp to become \"unsynchronized\" to the leader ramp.  At any time, the ramps can be re-synchronized by sending a trigger to in 3.  Holding a nonzero signal at input 3 will cause hard-sync type behaviour.\n\nIn this patch, a sync is \"requested\" whenever the div parameter changes significantly, or, when a reset input is triggered. This request is not serviced immediately, but held (via go.latchsync) until the master ramp cycles.  If you are using a ramp as a tempo clock, this means the sync will happen at the start of the next beat/bar/etc. \n\nThe sync applied here tries to minimize change: the current ramp is shifted to the nearest possible sync location. When div < 1, this is just like a hard sync that jumps toward zero, but when div > 1, which means the output ramp has a longer cycle than the input ramp, there could be more than one possible sync value. In this patch, the [round] operator will make it jump to whichever viable sync value is nearest. \n\nIf the autosync parameter is enabled (any nonzero value), then synchronization will also happen whenever the division input makes a sudden change. That way, a continuous modulation of the division input will create smoothly free-running followers, but snapping the division value to a new value will resynchronize the ramps. "
												}

											}
